WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.726
Welcome back to the explainer.

2
00:00:01.726 --> 00:00:06.617
Today we're diving head first into the fine print of property and casualty insurance.

3
00:00:06.617 --> 00:00:16.687
We're using the standard ISO forms to give you a really rigorous comprehensive look at the exact language that dictates whether your greatest assets are actually protected or,

4
00:00:16.687 --> 00:00:18.988
you know, completely vulnerable to ruin.

5
00:00:18.988 --> 00:00:22.556
Now, you're going to notice a ton of text on the screen today.

6
00:00:22.556 --> 00:00:30.612
We're putting as many words up there as possible because to really master these complex policies, you need to literally read the fine print.

7
00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:32.280
So, let's decode it together.

8
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.582
Here is our road map for this explainer.

9
00:00:34.582 --> 00:00:37.689
Section one, the fundamentals of homeowners insurance.

10
00:00:37.689 --> 00:00:40.796
Section two, homeowners exclusions and coverage costs.

11
00:00:40.796 --> 00:00:44.019
Section three, personal liability and umbrella policies.

12
00:00:44.019 --> 00:00:46.263
Section four, the personal auto policy.

13
00:00:46.263 --> 00:00:49.888
And finally, section five, auto exclusions and physical damage.

14
00:00:49.888 --> 00:00:55.182
All right, let's kick things off with section one, the fundamentals of homeowners insurance.

15
00:00:55.182 --> 00:00:57.828
Okay, look at this massive table we've put up.

16
00:00:57.828 --> 00:01:03.007
It's incredibly dense, I know, but it perfectly maps out how standard ISO forms are built.

17
00:01:03.007 --> 00:01:05.827
Notice how it's divided into four main coverages.

18
00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:10.833
A for dwelling, B for other structures, C for personal property, and D for loss of use.

19
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:13.882
Now, look closely at the popular HO3 and HO5 columns.

20
00:01:13.882 --> 00:01:17.220
See how they upgrade your coverage A to open peril status?

21
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.370
That is a huge deal.

22
00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:25.851
It essentially means you're covered against absolutely everything unless it's explicitly written out and excluded in the contract.

23
00:01:25.851 --> 00:01:30.166
To really understand your protection, read the specific text on this slide.

24
00:01:30.166 --> 00:01:34.194
The distinction between basic and broad coverage is insanely specific.

25
00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:38.740
Basic explicitly lists 12 named perils, things like fire, lightning, and theft.

26
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.897
Broad coverage upgrades that by adding six exact perils like falling objects, and the freezing of plumbing.

27
00:01:44.897 --> 00:01:46.163
And here's the kicker.

28
00:01:46.163 --> 00:01:51.974
If a disaster isn't explicitly named right here in the text, a basic policy simply will not cover it.

29
00:01:51.974 --> 00:01:56.347
Moving right along to section two, homeowners exclusions and coverage costs.

30
00:01:56.347 --> 00:01:58.707
Let's see what is definitely not covered.

31
00:01:58.707 --> 00:02:04.691
So, you read your policy and see that it covers your personal property at 50% of your dwelling coverage.

32
00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.590
Sounds pretty comfortable, right?

33
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:11.078
If you have a $400,000 home, you've got $200,000 for your belongings on paper.

34
00:02:11.078 --> 00:02:14.933
But wait, there is a major catch when it comes to high-value items.

35
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:17.522
Scan this giant list of limits on the screen.

36
00:02:17.522 --> 00:02:19.421
It's honestly a bit of a shocker.

37
00:02:19.421 --> 00:02:21.665
You get just 200 bucks for stolen cash.

38
00:02:21.665 --> 00:02:28.282
I mean, that barely covers a trip to the grocery store these days and only $1,500 for stolen jewelry or watercraft.

39
00:02:28.282 --> 00:02:35.993
If your valuables exceed these exact dollar limits written here, you absolutely have to add a scheduled personal property endorsement.

40
00:02:35.993 --> 00:02:38.582
And then there are the absolute dealbreakers.

41
00:02:38.582 --> 00:02:41.689
Read through these eight universal general exclusions.

42
00:02:41.689 --> 00:02:44.336
Standard policies will flat out deny you here.

43
00:02:44.336 --> 00:02:47.501
Earth movement, that means earthquakes are totally out.

44
00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:54.463
Water damage specifically refers to external flooding, which means you're going to need a separate national flood policy.

45
00:02:54.463 --> 00:02:59.527
They also explicitly exclude off-premises power failures, neglect, and intentional loss.

46
00:02:59.527 --> 00:03:02.404
Now, understanding valuation is totally essential.

47
00:03:02.404 --> 00:03:05.569
Check out this definition for actual cash value or ACV.

48
00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:10.575
It means the cost to replace an item minus depreciation based on its condition and age.

49
00:03:10.575 --> 00:03:11.438
Think about it.

50
00:03:11.438 --> 00:03:17.019
If your 10-year-old roof burns off, ACV only pays out the depreciated value of a decade old roof.

51
00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:21.680
And let's be real, that is almost never enough to actually build a brand new one.

52
00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.629
On the flip side, we have replacement cost value, which is the absolute gold standard.

53
00:03:26.629 --> 00:03:28.125
Just read that definition.

54
00:03:28.125 --> 00:03:34.109
It pays to rebuild your home as is using modern construction costs with zero deduction for depreciation.

55
00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:41.071
But to get this amazing coverage, insurers require you to insure the home for at least 80% of its total replacement cost.

56
00:03:41.071 --> 00:03:46.193
If you drop below that 80% threshold, it triggers what's known as a co-insurance penalty.

57
00:03:46.193 --> 00:03:48.955
We laid out the exact formula here step by step.

58
00:03:48.955 --> 00:03:56.492
The insurance company takes what you carried, divides it by the 80% you should have carried, multiplies that fraction by your loss,

59
00:03:56.492 --> 00:03:58.679
and finally subtracts your deductible.

60
00:03:58.679 --> 00:04:04.491
Basically, if you underinsure your home, you legally become a co-insurer for your own partial losses.

61
00:04:04.491 --> 00:04:05.296
No, thank you.

62
00:04:05.296 --> 00:04:09.151
All right, section three, personal liability and umbrella policies.

63
00:04:09.151 --> 00:04:11.568
Let's talk about defending your net worth.

64
00:04:11.568 --> 00:04:18.243
Read this text comparison carefully because the legal distinction between these two liability coverages is critical.

65
00:04:18.243 --> 00:04:25.263
Coverage E, personal liability, requires you to be legally liable for bodily injury, like say your dog bites a pedestrian.

66
00:04:25.263 --> 00:04:30.384
Coverage F, on the other hand, pays medical bills for injured guests regardless of fault.

67
00:04:30.384 --> 00:04:37.116
It acts as a quick goodwill payment to handle minor injuries without dragging everyone into a messy courtroom battle.

68
00:04:37.116 --> 00:04:44.827
Your personal liability coverage is definitely broad, but it hits a literal brick wall with these eight strict exclusions listed here.

69
00:04:44.827 --> 00:04:47.531
Make a massive mistake as a lawyer or a doctor.

70
00:04:47.531 --> 00:04:50.350
Your homeowner's policy is not going to save you.

71
00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:58.234
As you can read here, it also strictly excludes motor vehicles, aircraft, watercraft, and even the transmission of communicable diseases.

72
00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:03.297
So, what happens when a devastating lawsuit goes right past your standard policy limits?

73
00:05:03.297 --> 00:05:08.188
That is exactly where the PLUP or personal liability umbrella policy comes into play.

74
00:05:08.188 --> 00:05:12.561
It requires specific underlying limits on your home and auto policies first.

75
00:05:12.561 --> 00:05:15.668
When those initial limits run dry, the PLUP swoops in.

76
00:05:15.668 --> 00:05:24.300
Your primary policies basically act as a massive deductible before this huge drop-down million-dollar umbrella opens up to protect your future income.

77
00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:29.421
Let's shift gears and head out onto the road with section four, the personal auto policy.

78
00:05:29.421 --> 00:05:33.909
Did you know liability coverage is actually the only mandatory auto insurance?

79
00:05:33.909 --> 00:05:40.468
It's true because frankly the state cares way more about you protecting other people than protecting your own car.

80
00:05:40.468 --> 00:05:43.403
This is often expressed in split limits like 25/50.

81
00:05:43.403 --> 00:05:44.496
Read the breakdown.

82
00:05:44.496 --> 00:05:48.352
Those numbers dictate the exact maximum payout to the other driver.

83
00:05:48.352 --> 00:05:52.782
25 grand per person, 50 grand per accident, and 10 grand for property damage.

84
00:05:52.782 --> 00:05:57.903
Now, every standard auto policy is essentially a matrix made of these six distinct parts.

85
00:05:57.903 --> 00:06:00.262
We've got them all listed out right here.

86
00:06:00.262 --> 00:06:02.564
Part A handles your liability to others.

87
00:06:02.564 --> 00:06:05.959
Part B covers medical payments for you and your passengers.

88
00:06:05.959 --> 00:06:08.433
Part C protects you from uninsured drivers.

89
00:06:08.433 --> 00:06:10.850
Part D covers physical damage to your car.

90
00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.612
Part E dictates your exact duties after a crash.

91
00:06:13.612 --> 00:06:16.374
And part F handles the general legal provisions.

92
00:06:16.374 --> 00:06:18.330
So, how do they price all of this?

93
00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:20.747
Look at this list of underwriting factors.

94
00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:26.098
Insurers use every single one of these data points to mathematically calculate your premiums.

95
00:06:26.098 --> 00:06:27.997
They look at your age and gender.

96
00:06:27.997 --> 00:06:31.047
Single drivers under 25 are paying the absolute most.

97
00:06:31.047 --> 00:06:36.801
They also factor in your commute, your geographic location, your driving record, and in most states,

98
00:06:36.801 --> 00:06:42.728
even your credit score since statistical models heavily link that to your propensity for having a loss.

99
00:06:42.728 --> 00:06:47.618
Finally, let's wrap things up with section five, auto exclusions and physical damage.

100
00:06:47.618 --> 00:06:52.855
The textbook distinction between collision and comprehensive damage is absolutely critical,

101
00:06:52.855 --> 00:06:56.250
which is why we've put these text definitions side by side.

102
00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.760
Collision triggers when your car hits an object or overturns.

103
00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.363
Comprehensive covers non-collision perils like fire, theft, hail, and vandalism.

104
00:07:04.363 --> 00:07:05.916
Here's the classic example.

105
00:07:05.916 --> 00:07:09.369
If you swerve off the road and hit a tree, that's collision.

106
00:07:09.369 --> 00:07:13.857
But if a deer suddenly jumps out and hits you, that falls under comprehensive.

107
00:07:13.857 --> 00:07:20.589
But be warned, just like with the homeowner's policies, your coverage can instantly vanish under specific exclusions.

108
00:07:20.589 --> 00:07:21.452
Scan this list.

109
00:07:21.452 --> 00:07:27.839
If you are carrying persons for a fee, like driving for Uber or Lyft, without a special commercial endorsement,

110
00:07:27.839 --> 00:07:29.623
you are completely unprotected.

111
00:07:29.623 --> 00:07:36.586
The exact same rule applies if you're injured on a motorcycle, operating a car without entitlement, or racing on a track.

112
00:07:36.586 --> 00:07:38.945
And what if an accident actually happens?

113
00:07:38.945 --> 00:07:43.951
Well, your coverage fully relies on you following this exact checklist of party duties.

114
00:07:43.951 --> 00:07:46.195
Read every word of this list carefully.

115
00:07:46.195 --> 00:07:52.755
You're contractually obligated to provide prompt notice, cooperate in investigations, hand over all legal notices,

116
00:07:52.755 --> 00:07:56.840
authorize medical record exams, and notify the police of a hit and run.

117
00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:02.134
If you fail to comply with these exact words, you can legally void your protection entirely.

118
00:08:02.134 --> 00:08:02.364
Wow.

119
00:08:02.364 --> 00:08:07.140
Okay, we have covered a massive amount of dense, word heavy technical detail today,

120
00:08:07.140 --> 00:08:10.765
straight from the foundational texts of the insurance industry.

121
00:08:10.765 --> 00:08:14.217
And it leaves us with one incredibly vital question for you.

122
00:08:14.217 --> 00:08:18.015
Have you rigorously audited and coordinated your homeowners, auto,

123
00:08:18.015 --> 00:08:23.654
and umbrella policies to ensure there are no catastrophic wealth destroying gaps in your coverage?

124
00:08:23.654 --> 00:08:26.531
Knowing the definitions we read today is step one.

125
00:08:26.531 --> 00:08:28.545
Actually applying them is step two.

126
00:08:28.545 --> 00:08:30.156
So go check your fine print.

127
00:08:30.156 --> 00:08:34.299
Thanks for diving deep with us, and we'll see you on the next explainer.

